# Worked solutions, chapter 2 (time response analysis) part 3: 2.99 - 2.115, and chapter 3 part 1: 3.21 - 3.43
S = {
"2.99": ("B", "Error constants of a type-1 system.", "One integrator: type 1. $K_p=\\infty$, so the step error is 0.\n$K_v=\\lim_{s\\to0}sG=1$, so the ramp error is $\\dfrac1{K_v}=1$.\n\n$0$ and $1$: **(b)**."),
"2.100": ("B", "Extrema of the step response occur at $n\\pi/\\omega_d$.", "The response first overshoots at $\\pi/\\omega_d$ and then undershoots at $2\\pi/\\omega_d$: **(b)**."),
"2.101": ("D", "A type-1 system cannot follow a parabola.", "A parabolic input needs $K_a=\\lim_{s\\to0}s^2G$. For a type-1 system $K_a=0$, so $e_{ss}=\\dfrac1{K_a}=\\infty$: **(d)**."),
"2.102": ("A", "When a lower-order approximation is valid.", "A second-order approximation is justified when no uncancelled zero sits near the dominant pole pair, i.e. a zero near the pair is nearly cancelled by a close higher-order pole, and the remaining higher-order poles do not matter: **(a)**."),
"2.103": ("D", "Superposition: step part $+$ ramp part.", "Type 1: $K_v=\\lim_{s\\to0}sG=K$ and $K_p=\\infty$.\nFor $r=1+5t$: $e_{ss}=\\dfrac1{1+K_p}+\\dfrac5{K_v}=0+\\dfrac5K<0.1\\Rightarrow K>50$: **(d)**."),
"2.104": ("D", "Type 1: the error is for a unit ramp, $e_{ss}=1/K_v$.", "$K_v=\\lim_{s\\to0}sG=\\dfrac{5K}{6\\cdot7\\cdot8}=\\dfrac{5K}{336}$.\n$e_{ss}=\\dfrac1{K_v}=0.1\\Rightarrow K_v=10\\Rightarrow K=\\dfrac{3360}{5}=672$: **(d)**."),
"2.105": ("A", "Definition of rise time.", "The time to go from 10% to 90% of the final value (0 to 100% for underdamped systems) is the rise time: **(a)**."),
"2.106": ("A", "Damping ratio varies as $1/\\sqrt K$.", "$1+G=0\\Rightarrow\\tau s^2+s+K=0$, so $\\omega_n^2=K/\\tau$ and $2\\xi\\omega_n=1/\\tau$.\n$\\xi=\\dfrac1{2\\sqrt{K\\tau}}\\propto\\dfrac1{\\sqrt K}$.\n$\\dfrac{\\xi_2}{\\xi_1}=3=\\sqrt{K_1/K_2}\\Rightarrow\\dfrac{K_2}{K_1}=\\dfrac19=0.1111$: **(a)**."),
"2.107": ("D", "Reduce the inner loop, then use $K_v$.", "Inner loop: $\\dfrac{10/s}{1+0.1\\cdot10/s}=\\dfrac{10}{s+1}$.\nOuter loop (tachometer gain 1): $G(s)=\\dfrac Ks\\cdot\\dfrac{10}{s+1}$, a type-1 system with $K_v=10K$.\nRamp error $\\dfrac1{10K}<0.01\\Rightarrow K>10$.\n\nOf the options only $14$ satisfies this: **(d)**."),
"2.108": ("B", "Definitions of the time-response specifications.", "1. This describes *rise time*, not delay time: incorrect.\n2. Peak time is the time to the first peak overshoot: correct.\n3. Settling time uses a 2% or 5% band of the *final* value, not 3% or 5% of the initial value: incorrect.\n\nNot correct: 1 and 3: **(b)**."),
"2.109": ("B", "$t_s(5\\%)=3/(\\xi\\omega_n)$.", "$\\omega_n=5,\\ 2\\xi\\omega_n=8\\Rightarrow\\xi\\omega_n=4$.\n$t_s=\\dfrac34$ s: **(b)**."),
"2.110": ("D", "Read $\\sigma$ and $\\omega_d$ from the response.", "The decay rate is $\\xi\\omega_n=1$ and $\\omega_d=\\sqrt3$, so $\\omega_n^2=1+3=4$ and $\\xi=0.5$.\nThe amplitude $\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-\\xi^2}}=\\dfrac2{\\sqrt3}$ and phase $\\cos^{-1}\\xi=60^\\circ$ confirm it.\n\n$T(s)=\\dfrac4{s^2+2s+4}$: **(d)**."),
"2.111": ("C", "Critically damped step response $c=1-(1+t)e^{-t}$.", "$G=\\dfrac1{(s+1)^2}$ gives $c(t)=1-(1+t)e^{-t}$.\nSet $c=0.94$: $(1+t)e^{-t}=0.06$. Solving, $t\\approx4.52$ s.\n\nThe nearest option is $4.50$: **(c)**."),
"2.112": ("D", "Peaks (maxima) of the step response occur at $\\pi/\\omega_d,\\ 3\\pi/\\omega_d,\\dots$", "$\\omega_n=5,\\ \\xi=0.8,\\ \\omega_d=5\\sqrt{1-0.64}=3$ rad/s.\nFirst peak: $\\pi/3$; second peak: $3\\pi/3=\\pi$ s: **(d)**."),
"2.113": ("C", "Error constants of $\\dfrac{10}{s(1+0.1s)}$.", "$K_p=\\infty$ (one integrator), $K_v=\\lim sG=10$, $K_a=\\lim s^2G=0$.\nIn the order acceleration, velocity, position: $0,\\ 10,\\ \\infty$: **(c)**."),
"2.114": ("D", "$t_p=\\pi/\\omega_d$.", "$s^2+10s+K$: $2\\xi\\omega_n=10$ with $\\xi=0.5$ gives $\\omega_n=10$.\n$\\omega_d=10\\sqrt{1-0.25}=8.66$ rad/s.\n$t_p=\\dfrac\\pi{8.66}=0.363$ s: **(d)**."),
"2.115": ("B", "Definitions of the time-response specifications.", "1. Rise time is 10% to 90% (or 0 to 100% for underdamped systems), not 5% to 95%: incorrect.\n2. Maximum overshoot indicates relative stability: correct.\n3. The settling band is 2% or 5% of the final value, not 3% or 6%: incorrect.\n\nNot correct: 1 and 3: **(b)**."),
"3.21": ("D", "Routh array: marginal stability when the $s^1$ element is zero.", "| $s^4$ | 1 | 4 | $K$ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| $s^3$ | 4 | 3 | |\n| $s^2$ | $13/4$ | $K$ | |\n| $s^1$ | $\\dfrac{39/4-4K}{13/4}$ | | |\n\nMarginal stability: the $s^1$ term is zero: $4K=\\dfrac{39}{4}\\Rightarrow K=\\dfrac{39}{16}$: **(d)**."),
"3.22": ("B", "Characteristic equation: $1+G(s)H(s)=0$.", "Forward path: $4\\cdot\\dfrac3{s+1}=\\dfrac{12}{s+1}$; feedback $H=\\dfrac{10}{s+10}$.\n$1+\\dfrac{120}{(s+1)(s+10)}=0\\Rightarrow s^2+11s+10+120=0$, i.e. $s^2+11s+130=0$: **(b)**."),
"3.23": ("D", "Routh array: the $s^1$ element must be positive.", "| $s^4$ | 1 | 24 | $K$ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| $s^3$ | 8 | 32 | |\n| $s^2$ | 20 | $K$ | |\n| $s^1$ | $32-0.4K$ | | |\n\nStable for $0<K<80$. For $K=100$ the $s^1$ element is negative: unstable: **(d)**."),
"3.24": ("A", "A zero row in the Routh array gives imaginary roots.", "| $s^3$ | 1 | 6 |\n|---|---|---|\n| $s^2$ | 2 | 12 |\n| $s^1$ | 0 | |\n\nThe auxiliary equation $2s^2+12=0$ gives $s=\\pm j\\sqrt6$, a purely imaginary pair, so $\\delta=0$: **(a)**."),
"3.25": ("B", "Routh criterion on $s^3+3s^2+2s+K$.", "$1+G=0\\Rightarrow s^3+3s^2+2s+K=0$.\nThe $s^1$ element is $\\dfrac{6-K}{3}>0\\Rightarrow K<6$, and $K>0$.\n\n$0<K<6$: **(b)**."),
"3.26": ("C", "Gain margin $=20\\log\\dfrac{K_{crit}}{K}$.", "$GM=20\\log\\dfrac{40}{20}=20\\log2=6$ dB: **(c)**."),
"3.27": ("D", "Routh array with a gain-dependent term.", "| $s^4$ | 1 | 5 | 15 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| $s^3$ | $20K$ | 10 | |\n| $s^2$ | $5-\\dfrac{0.5}{K}$ | 15 | |\n| $s^1$ | $10-\\dfrac{300K}{b}$ | | |\n\nwhere $b=5-\\dfrac{0.5}{K}$. Positivity of the first column needs $b>0$ ($K>0.1$) and $300K^2-50K+5<0$, whose discriminant is $2500-6000<0$, so that never holds.\n\nThe system is unstable for all $K$: **(d)**."),
"3.28": ("C", "Routh criterion on $s^3+4s^2+s+(K-6)$.", "Conditions: $K-6>0$ and $4\\cdot1>K-6\\Rightarrow K<10$.\n\n$6<K<10$: **(c)**."),
"3.29": ("C", "Sustained oscillation means marginal stability.", "$s^3+6s^2+5s+K=0$. The $s^1$ element is $\\dfrac{30-K}{6}$; it is zero at $K=30$, where the poles are on the $j\\omega$ axis ($\\omega=\\sqrt5$): **(c)**."),
"3.30": ("C", "Routh array: sign changes equal RHP roots.", "| $s^4$ | 2 | 3 | 7 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| $s^3$ | 1 | 5 | |\n| $s^2$ | $-7$ | 7 | |\n| $s^1$ | 6 | | |\n| $s^0$ | 7 | | |\n\nFirst column: $2,\\ 1,\\ -7,\\ 6,\\ 7$ has two sign changes, so two roots lie in the right half-plane: **(c)**."),
"3.31": ("A", "Phase margin and stability.", "For a stable feedback system (the usual minimum-phase case) the phase margin is positive. A negative phase margin indicates instability: **(a)**."),
"3.32": ("A", "Effects of negative feedback.", "Negative feedback can improve stability and reduces the sensitivity of the response to parameter variations and external disturbances: **(a)**."),
"3.33": ("C", "Type = number of poles at the origin; order = highest power of $s$.", "$G=\\dfrac K{s^2(1+sT)}$ has two poles at the origin: type 2.\nThe denominator is third degree: order 3.\n\n**(c)**."),
"3.34": ("C", "Auxiliary equation gives the oscillation frequency.", "The Routh array gives a zero row at $s^3$. The auxiliary equation from the $s^4$ row is $s^4+2s^2+1=(s^2+1)^2=0$, so $s=\\pm j1$ (repeated).\nThe oscillation frequency is 1 rad/s, i.e. the roots $\\pm j1$: **(c)**."),
"3.35": ("D", "Same characteristic equation as 3.27.", "The Routh array has first-column entries $20K,\\ 5-\\dfrac{0.5}{K},\\ 10-\\dfrac{300K}{5-0.5/K}$. Requiring all to be positive needs $300K^2-50K+5<0$, which has no real solution.\n\nUnstable for any $K$: **(d)**."),
"3.36": ("C", "Routh criterion on $s^4+4s^3+13s^2+36s+K$.", "| $s^4$ | 1 | 13 | $K$ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| $s^3$ | 4 | 36 | |\n| $s^2$ | 4 | $K$ | |\n| $s^1$ | $36-K$ | | |\n\nStable for $K>0$ and $36-K>0$: $0<K<36$: **(c)**."),
"3.37": ("B", "Characteristic equation of $G=\\dfrac{K(s+1)(s+2)}{(s+0.1)(s-1)}$.", "$(s+0.1)(s-1)+K(s^2+3s+2)=(1+K)s^2+(3K-0.9)s+(2K-0.1)=0$.\nThe constant term is negative for $K<0.05$, so the product of the roots is negative: one positive real root and one negative one.\nFor $0.05<K<0.3$ the middle coefficient is negative, so both roots are in the RHP, and for $K>0.3$ the system is stable.\n\nExactly one RHP pole: $K<0.05$: **(b)**."),
"3.38": ("D", "Routh criterion on $s^3+(2+K)s^2+2Ks+10$.", "Conditions: $2+K>0$, $K>0$, and $(2+K)(2K)>10\\Rightarrow K^2+2K-5>0\\Rightarrow K>-1+\\sqrt6=1.449$.\n\nStable for $K>1.45$: **(d)**."),
"3.39": ("C", "Open loop has a double pole at the origin; check the closed loop with Routh.", "1. Open loop: $\\dfrac{K(s+1)}{s^2(s+2)}$ has a repeated pole at the origin, so it is unstable.\n2. Closed loop: $s^3+2s^2+Ks+K=0$. The $s^1$ element is $\\dfrac{2K-K}{2}=\\dfrac K2>0$, so it is stable for $K>0$.\n\n1 is unstable and 2 is stable: **(c)**."),
"3.40": ("B", "Routh criterion on $s^4+20s^3+15s^2+2s+K$.", "| $s^4$ | 1 | 15 | $K$ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| $s^3$ | 20 | 2 | |\n| $s^2$ | 14.9 | $K$ | |\n| $s^1$ | $2-\\dfrac{20K}{14.9}$ | | |\n\n$s^1>0\\Rightarrow K<1.49$, and $K>0$.\n\n$0<K<1.49$: **(b)**."),
"3.41": ("A", "Stability definition.", "All characteristic roots in the left half-plane means the system is stable: **(a)**."),
"3.42": ("D", "Instability conditions.", "A root in the right half-plane, or repeated roots on the $j\\omega$ axis, makes the response grow without bound: unstable: **(d)**."),
"3.43": ("C", "Characteristic equation and the Routh condition.", "$s(s^2+5s+9)+K(1-s)=s^3+5s^2+(9-K)s+K=0$.\nThe $s^1$ element is $\\dfrac{5(9-K)-K}{5}>0\\Rightarrow K<7.5$, and $K>0$.\n\nThe maximum $K$ is $7.5$: **(c)**."),
}
